WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.003 --> 00:00:02.017
Today we're going to create a typographic animation

00:00:02.018 --> 00:00:05.018
entirely driven by physics-based
collisions in After Effects,

00:00:05.393 --> 00:00:06.368
using the Newton plugin

00:00:06.393 --> 00:00:09.370
and a custom expression built
with help from an AI assistant.

00:00:09.444 --> 00:00:11.088
I came up with a list of short words,

00:00:11.113 --> 00:00:14.441
4 or 5 letters max, that
express pain or impact.

00:00:14.466 --> 00:00:16.988
We mixed onomatopoeias
like "Ouch" and "Yikes".

00:00:17.055 --> 00:00:21.227
Pain sounds like "Ugh", and soft
swear words like "D*mn" or "Sh*t".

00:00:21.359 --> 00:00:22.650
The goal was to use short,

00:00:22.675 --> 00:00:25.469
expressive, punchy words that
feel natural and readable in motion.

00:00:25.494 --> 00:00:27.938
The first word displayed will be: "Weeee".

00:00:28.483 --> 00:00:30.283
I start by creating a new composition.

00:00:30.933 --> 00:00:33.383
I create a text layer
and type this first word.

00:00:34.283 --> 00:00:35.738
I center its anchor point,

00:00:37.637 --> 00:00:39.325
then draw a mask around the text.

00:00:39.350 --> 00:00:42.055
This mask keeps Newton from
interpreting the full text geometry,

00:00:42.079 --> 00:00:43.922
simplifying the simulation.

00:00:43.923 --> 00:00:46.940
Since we can't animate fonts directly
in Newton, keeping a defined space

00:00:46.941 --> 00:00:49.941
lets us switch out the words
without altering the physical behavior.

00:00:49.945 --> 00:00:52.491
Using this simple setup, I
can now test the process.

00:00:52.516 --> 00:00:53.788
Let's launch Newton.

00:00:53.813 --> 00:00:55.300
In Newton, I first add a ground.

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:58.915
What I want is to capture all
the collision data from my object

00:00:58.937 --> 00:01:00.942
whenever it hits a wall or another body.

00:01:00.943 --> 00:01:02.928
I want that interaction recorded.

00:01:02.929 --> 00:01:08.327
I select the object, go to Body Properties
&gt; Advanced, and enable Export Contacts.

00:01:10.027 --> 00:01:13.922
I increase the solver substeps
and lower the collision tolerance.

00:01:13.935 --> 00:01:15.916
Then I run a quick 100-frame test.

00:01:17.399 --> 00:01:18.541
In the new composition,

00:01:18.566 --> 00:01:20.967
I can see the simulation

00:01:21.931 --> 00:01:23.933
and more importantly, I now have an effect

00:01:23.934 --> 00:01:26.946
called Contact with a slider and
keyframes that match the collisions.

00:01:27.922 --> 00:01:28.949
They're not always super precise,

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:31.950
and sometimes the collision
happens between two frames.

00:01:31.959 --> 00:01:33.923
Another useful detail:

00:01:33.924 --> 00:01:36.150
Newton also tracks the
energy of the contact.

00:01:37.238 --> 00:01:38.924
Here's how the expression works.

00:01:38.925 --> 00:01:40.950
It contains the list of words we created.

00:01:40.951 --> 00:01:42.945
The first word shown is always "Weee".

00:01:43.206 --> 00:01:46.666
It reads the slider values from the
Contact effect created by Export Contacts.

00:01:47.206 --> 00:01:49.205
It also includes two filters.

00:01:49.206 --> 00:01:51.915
The first filter ignores collisions
that are too close together,

00:01:51.916 --> 00:01:53.205
less than three frames apart.

00:01:53.572 --> 00:01:55.944
The second ignores small changes under 30
units

00:01:55.969 --> 00:01:57.605
to focus only on meaningful impacts.

00:01:57.895 --> 00:02:00.494
I copy the expression and
apply it to the text source.

00:02:01.206 --> 00:02:02.516
Let's see what it does.

00:02:04.015 --> 00:02:05.015
It works.

00:02:05.105 --> 00:02:07.582
Though I need to change the
blending mode of the text layer

00:02:07.606 --> 00:02:09.707
to None to make it visible.

00:02:09.749 --> 00:02:13.400
Now we can see that the word
changes at every valid collision.

00:02:14.206 --> 00:02:15.588
Let's scale things up.

00:02:16.206 --> 00:02:17.206
I delete the test comp.

00:02:19.577 --> 00:02:22.655
I reset the layer’s mode to "None"
again and paste the expression.

00:02:23.499 --> 00:02:26.499
Then I duplicate the text layer 50 times.

00:02:28.027 --> 00:02:29.623
I offset each layer by three frames

00:02:29.624 --> 00:02:31.461
using the Sequence Layers command.

00:02:36.133 --> 00:02:39.400
Then I randomize the scale
between 20% and 120%.

00:02:40.836 --> 00:02:42.255
I relaunch Newton.

00:02:43.572 --> 00:02:45.722
I place walls on the left and right.

00:02:46.818 --> 00:02:48.982
Then I use the Randomizer
to distribute the layer positions

00:02:49.007 --> 00:02:52.007
between 100 and 900 pixels.

00:02:57.206 --> 00:03:01.205
I randomize the rotation between -10
and +10 degrees.

00:03:01.472 --> 00:03:02.716
Let's preview that.

00:03:03.816 --> 00:03:04.994
Not bad,

00:03:05.316 --> 00:03:07.783
but I add some gravity
to make it more dynamic.

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:11.623
Much better.

00:03:11.624 --> 00:03:14.624
Next I randomize more
physical properties: density,

00:03:16.572 --> 00:03:17.538
friction,

00:03:18.145 --> 00:03:19.207
bounciness,

00:03:21.457 --> 00:03:23.244
and angular velocity.

00:03:28.049 --> 00:03:31.049
Now the animation looks much more lively.

00:03:32.981 --> 00:03:35.020
All that's left is to render the final comp.

00:03:35.206 --> 00:03:36.611
And here's the result.

00:03:37.749 --> 00:03:40.205
In the end, it was fast
and simple to build,

00:03:40.206 --> 00:03:43.499
and the animation is expressive,
physical, and interactive

00:03:44.077 --> 00:03:47.205
If you enjoyed this breakdown, feel
free to subscribe or drop a comment.

00:03:47.206 --> 00:03:50.205
More Newton plus expressions
experiments are coming soon.

00:03:50.505 --> 00:03:52.505
Thanks for watching and see you next time.

